# Islands, hydration, and accessibility

## Browser APIs live inside effects — hydration is sacred

`window`, `document`, `navigator`, and DOM measurement run only inside
`useEffect`, `useLayoutEffect`, or event handlers. Never at module scope, in a
component body, or in a `useMemo` initialiser.

The first client render matches the SSR HTML byte-for-byte. Sources of
mismatch and their fixes:

1. `Math.random()` / `Date.now()` during render → move to an effect or
   pre-compute on the server and pass as a prop.
2. Wall-clock reads (`new Date().getFullYear()`) → thread as a prop from a
   server component.
3. `typeof window !== 'undefined'` branches that change output → keep the
   gating value `null` through SSR and the first client render, flip after
   hydration.
4. Locale-sensitive formatting → format on the server with an explicit locale.

Use event handlers, derived render state, refs, or the
derive-state-while-rendering form. A closed portal may read `document.body`
during client render when the target does not affect output until interaction.

Layout reads (`getBoundingClientRect`, `offsetWidth`, `scrollHeight`) run in
`useLayoutEffect`.

## Forms stay small

A form is an uncontrolled `<form>` (or a single controlled input when the
value drives a typeahead) whose submit handler reads the values. The network
call, response validation, and the `idle | loading | ready | error` lifecycle
live in one hook the form consumes; the form decides what the states look like.
Validation lives with the form definition, not as ad-hoc guards in the handler.
Every form works without JavaScript: a real `action`/`method` that lands on
the same destination.

Submissions go through the Keystone route handlers (`app/api/form`,
`app/api/chat`) or the documented external API; never a hand-rolled endpoint.

## Accessibility baseline

- Meaningful `alt` on every image; `alt=""` for decorative and ambient
  photography.
- Every interactive element is keyboard-navigable and has a visible
  `:focus-visible` style.
- `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` renders every state directly. Auto-rotation
  pauses on hover and focus; timers run only in-viewport with the document
  visible.
- Color contrast meets WCAG AA (4.5:1 body, 3:1 large text and UI); report the
  measured ratios for new color pairs.
- Semantic HTML before ARIA: `<ul><li>`, `<button>`, `<nav>`, `<figure>` with
  `<blockquote>` and `<figcaption>` for testimonials. ARIA patterns
  (combobox/listbox, dialog) follow the WAI-ARIA authoring practices exactly:
  `role`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-controls`, `aria-activedescendant`,
  `aria-busy`, `aria-invalid` + `aria-describedby`, `role="alert"` for errors.
- No interactive `<div>`. A control is a `<button>` or `<a>`; style it as the
  design needs. The one exception is a modal backdrop whose `onClick` closes
  only when the target is the backdrop.
- Focus management: an overlay moves focus to its container
  (`tabIndex={-1}`), not the first input; on close focus returns to the
  opener. Use `focus({ preventScroll: true })` both ways. A menu row keeps the
  input's focus (`onMouseDown` prevents default) so the combobox pattern holds.
- Decorative SVGs are `aria-hidden="true"`; meaningful SVGs carry `role="img"`
  and a name. Lattice chrome, ornament cells, and clone slides are
  `aria-hidden`.
- Touch targets ≥ 44 × 44 px; every hover state also works on touch.
